WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.221
Hello and welcome to mCoding

00:00:01.221 --> 00:00:04.661
where we try&nbsp;to get just a little bit 
 better at programming every episode.

00:00:04.661 --> 00:00:06.173
I'm James Murphy.

00:00:06.173 --> 00:00:08.018
I'm using an&nbsp;experimental editor theme today.

00:00:08.018 --> 00:00:11.030
So let me know if it's better or worse than usual.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:14.688
Today we're&nbsp;talking about the 
 algorithm that I affectionately call "fast_pow."

00:00:14.688 --> 00:00:16.320
If you're a long-time viewer,&nbsp;you might remember that

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.392
I've had a video about efficient exponentiation before.

00:00:19.392 --> 00:00:22.793
That video&nbsp;is more about how to find the least 
 number of multiplications possible

00:00:22.793 --> 00:00:25.204
to raise something&nbsp;to a small power.

00:00:25.204 --> 00:00:29.947
"fast_pow," on the other hand, is a general 
 algorithm to raise things to very&nbsp;large powers.

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:32.597
Of course, if there's a "fast_pow," 
 there's probably a "slow_pow,".

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:35.640
And that's the one&nbsp;you'd 
 probably come up with on your first try.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.404
To raise x to the nth power, 
 we start with&nbsp;one and multiply by x n times.

00:00:41.404 --> 00:00:45.702
That means it takes n multiplies overall 
 in order to raise&nbsp;something to the nth power.

00:00:45.702 --> 00:00:52.216
The idea for "fast_pow" is that x to the n is 
 approximately equal to x to&nbsp;the n over 2 squared.

00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:54.867
If n is even, that's exactly correct.

00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:58.654
And if n is odd, then we just need&nbsp;
 to multiply by another single x.

00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:01.104
This is great because it phrases the 
 answer to the problem

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:05.403
"What's x to the n?" in terms of the 
 solution to "What's x to the n over 2?"

00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:07.243
A problem of half the size.

00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:10.629
This lends itself&nbsp;to a very 
 simple and elegant recursive solution.

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:13.447
So let's see how to turn this into "fast_pow."

00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:18.334
We start off with our base case, which is that 
 raising something to the zeroth power always gives&nbsp;1.

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:22.495
Feel free to argue in the comments  about  
  whether this does the right thing for x equals&nbsp;zero.

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:24.790
If you disagree, you could always raise an exception.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:28.568
We're going to need half the value of&nbsp;
 n and the remainder after division by 2.

00:01:28.568 --> 00:01:31.452
A bit of Python trivia, though: if you 
 need both the&nbsp;quotient and remainder,

00:01:31.452 --> 00:01:33.467
this is not the best way to do it.

00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:38.346
Python has this built-in called "divmod"&nbsp;that gives 
 you both the quotient and remainder at the same time.

00:01:38.346 --> 00:01:42.000
If you're wondering why Python&nbsp;would have a single 
 function that gives you both the quotient and remainder

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.146
instead of just&nbsp;expecting you to use 
 divide by 2 and then mod 2,

00:01:46.146 --> 00:01:49.720
a lot of integer long division 
 algorithms,&nbsp;like the one that you learned in school,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.808
produce the remainder as sort of just a 
 byproduct&nbsp;of the algorithm.

00:01:53.808 --> 00:01:59.221
So if you compute and divide by something, 
 you actually get n mod the same&nbsp;something for free.

00:01:59.221 --> 00:02:02.077
Of course, bitwise operations 
 also work since we're using two here.

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:06.505
But in&nbsp;general, even if you're not using 2, 
 "divmod" can give you both at the same time.

00:02:06.505 --> 00:02:10.244
Moving on,&nbsp;we  
recursively compute x to half the power,

00:02:10.244 --> 00:02:14.640
Then square that answer and multiply 
 by&nbsp;x one more time if there was a remainder.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.369
Otherwise, just return the result.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:20.305
It's simple,&nbsp;there's a great core idea. 
 It's very elegant.

00:02:20.305 --> 00:02:21.556
And it's pretty easy to code up.

00:02:21.556 --> 00:02:25.080
This is something&nbsp;you could 
 learn as your first recursive algorithm.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.426
By the way, other people call&nbsp;
 this algorithm "exponentiation&nbsp;by squaring,".

00:02:29.426 --> 00:02:33.000
But that's a pretty lame&nbsp;name. 
 So I'm going to call it "fast_pow."

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.732
So anyway, instead of using n multiplications,&nbsp;
 it only uses about log n multiplications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.732 --> 00:02:41.520
There are plenty of ways that you could 
 take&nbsp;this code and tweak it to go slightly faster.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.238
And that's not really the direction 
 that I want&nbsp;to take this in.

00:02:45.238 --> 00:02:49.122
After all, Python already has 
 a built-in exponentiation operator.

00:02:49.122 --> 00:02:53.428
And as you&nbsp;could probably guess,  
 "fast_pow" is not that fast compared to it.

00:02:53.428 --> 00:02:56.939
Although that's due in part&nbsp; 
 to the fact that the built-in exponentiation operator

00:02:56.939 --> 00:03:00.432
uses "fast_pow" or some 
 more sophisticated&nbsp;modification of it.

00:03:00.432 --> 00:03:02.609
Now, the direction that I want to take this in is:

00:03:02.609 --> 00:03:05.863
What if we allow x to be&nbsp;
 something other than an integer.

00:03:05.863 --> 00:03:08.715
And what if we allow a different kind of multiplication?

00:03:08.715 --> 00:03:14.190
This&nbsp;idea and this algorithm don't 
 really depend on integers or multiplication.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.480
And recognizing this&nbsp;allows 
 "fast_pow" to be a more general algorithm.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.684
We'll still stick to non-negative integer&nbsp;powers.

00:03:21.684 --> 00:03:25.001
But other than that, let's 
 see what else we actually used.

00:03:25.001 --> 00:03:29.491
So let's just parameterize&nbsp;this function 
 by the kind of multiplication that we want to do.

00:03:29.491 --> 00:03:33.960
We'll do this by adding two&nbsp; 
  new arguments: "multiply" and "identity."

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.700
We'll use "multiply" in place of anywhere&nbsp;
that we were going to use the star operator.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.920
And we'll use "identity" in place of 1&nbsp;
 because 1 was really specific to integers.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.900
So if we raise something to the zeroth 
 power,&nbsp;we need to return the identity instead of 1.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.957
The division here is division of the power n,&nbsp;not of x.

00:03:51.957 --> 00:03:54.660
So that is still integer division.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.545
We can still recursively use "fast_pow".

00:03:57.545 --> 00:04:00.360
But we can't use star equals here.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.046
Star equals is effectively just 
 saying result&nbsp;is result times result.

00:04:04.046 --> 00:04:06.060
That's squaring the value.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.500
So let's replace squaring the 
 value&nbsp;with multiplying the value with itself.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.140
Same thing goes here; instead&nbsp;
 of using the star operator to multiply,

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.700
we'll use the mul operator to multiply.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.560
And don't forget to pass the new arguments here.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.782
Now, in order to use our more&nbsp;general "fast_pow"

00:04:21.782 --> 00:04:25.589
we need to define this multiplication and identity.

00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:27.181
If we wanted to check our implementation,

00:04:27.181 --> 00:04:30.417
we could define our multiplication 
 function to&nbsp;just take two arguments

00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:32.710
and use the built-in multiplication.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.619
The identity for this&nbsp;
is the one that we had here before.

00:04:36.619 --> 00:04:39.532
Run it now and all of our asserts pass.

00:04:39.532 --> 00:04:42.000
1 to the power of 100 is 1.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.343
5 cubed is 125.

00:04:44.343 --> 00:04:47.248
And 2 to the 11th is 2048.

00:04:47.248 --> 00:04:52.174
So what other kinds of 
 multiplications and identity can I pass in?

00:04:52.174 --> 00:04:56.730
We just need a multiplication where 
 we're allowed to put parentheses wherever we want.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.518
That's called associativity.

00:04:58.518 --> 00:05:03.050
The other property that we used is that 
 multiplying by the identity doesn't change the value.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:07.317
We actually don't care about commutativity 
 because we're always multiplying by the same number.

00:05:07.317 --> 00:05:09.529
Reordering them doesn't change anything.

00:05:09.529 --> 00:05:13.032
So we need associativity and an identity element.

00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:18.343
Well instead of multiplication another associative 
 operator that we have is addition.

00:05:18.343 --> 00:05:22.211
It doesn't matter where you put the 
 parentheses when you do addition of integers.

00:05:22.211 --> 00:05:26.116
But one is not the element 
 that does nothing for addition.

00:05:26.116 --> 00:05:30.000
If we want adding something
 to do nothing, then we should use zero.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.372
Well, then what's "fast_pow" going 
 to give us in this case.

00:05:33.372 --> 00:05:37.487
1 to the power 100 is giving us 100.

00:05:37.487 --> 00:05:40.926
5 to the power 3 is giving us 15.

00:05:40.926 --> 00:05:44.467
And 2 to the 11 is giving us 22.

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:49.380
Read those numbers again and you realize 
 that it's actually just doing regular multiplication.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.235
That's right. "fast_pow" on addition gives multiplication.

00:05:53.235 --> 00:05:55.259
1 times a 100 is a 100.

00:05:55.259 --> 00:05:59.074
5 times 3 is 15 and 2 times 11 is 22.

00:05:59.074 --> 00:06:03.516
This is happening because although we 
 named it 'pow' for power like multiplication

00:06:03.516 --> 00:06:06.869
what we're really doing is repeated application.

00:06:06.869 --> 00:06:11.162
So repeated application of 
 multiplication is exponentiation.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:15.127
But repeated application 
 of addition is multiplication.

00:06:15.127 --> 00:06:19.947
If I repeatedly add 5 to itself 
 three times that's the same as 5 times 3.

00:06:19.947 --> 00:06:21.572
Let's test your intuition.

00:06:21.572 --> 00:06:26.228
If we still took multiplication 
 to mean the built-in plus operator

00:06:26.228 --> 00:06:29.741
And we took our identity to be the empty string,

00:06:29.741 --> 00:06:33.019
then what would the 
 empty string to the power of 100 be?

00:06:33.019 --> 00:06:35.935
What would 'a' to the 11 be?

00:06:35.935 --> 00:06:38.880
And what would 'abc' cubed be?

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.706
Answer coming in 3

00:06:40.706 --> 00:06:41.687
Answer coming in 3 2

00:06:41.687 --> 00:06:43.291
Answer coming in 3 2 1.

00:06:43.291 --> 00:06:47.061
The empty string to the 100 is the empty string.

00:06:47.061 --> 00:06:49.903
a to the 11 is 11 a's.

00:06:49.903 --> 00:06:53.828
And 'abc' cubed is just 'abcabcabc'.

00:06:53.828 --> 00:06:57.845
Once Again repeated addition is 
 basically giving us a form of multiplication.

00:06:57.845 --> 00:07:01.651
Multiplication for strings 
 means repeat this that many times.

00:07:01.651 --> 00:07:04.672
This is even consistent 
 with Python's built-in multiplication.

00:07:04.672 --> 00:07:08.954
So if you've ever wondered why a 
 hundred times the empty string is even allowed,

00:07:08.954 --> 00:07:14.395
Hopefully, this gives you an idea that it's 
 because there's some deeper underlying structure there.

00:07:14.395 --> 00:07:19.097
So, I mean cool story, bro. 
 But is this actually useful for anything?

00:07:19.096 --> 00:07:23.391
So far, we've seen a slower version of 
 the built-in exponentiation operator for integers.

00:07:23.391 --> 00:07:28.377
And an even slower version of the 
 multiplication operator for integers and for strings.

00:07:28.377 --> 00:07:31.533
There's pretty much no way that we're 
 going to be able to build the speed of a Python built-in

00:07:31.533 --> 00:07:33.661
using something in pure Python.

00:07:33.661 --> 00:07:38.491
So, we need to think about 
 expensive operations that are not built into Python.

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:41.445
You got it. Here's an example 
 I think you're really going to like.

00:07:41.445 --> 00:07:43.912
Let's start by importing NumPy.

00:07:43.912 --> 00:07:47.420
Then let's take our multiplication to be convolution.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:51.431
Convolution is an extremely important 
 operation especially in Signal processing.

00:07:51.431 --> 00:07:55.695
And it has a lot of the same 
 properties that the basic multiplication does.

00:07:55.695 --> 00:07:59.673
In particular, it is associative.

00:07:59.673 --> 00:08:01.913
But there's a problem.

00:08:01.913 --> 00:08:05.414
If you've ever looked at the 
 Wikipedia article for convolution,

00:08:05.414 --> 00:08:10.827
you'll see all kinds of mathematical jargon 
 about something called an approximation to the identity.

00:08:10.827 --> 00:08:15.000
Why doesn't Wikipedia just tell me 
 what the identity is for the multiplication?

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.808
Well, that's because there is no identity for convolution.

00:08:17.808 --> 00:08:19.763
At least not for real numbers.

00:08:19.763 --> 00:08:21.671
Now technically we're on a computer.

00:08:21.671 --> 00:08:24.955
And the discrete convolution does actually have an identity.

00:08:24.955 --> 00:08:28.232
But for this example, we're going 
 to be modeling something continuous.

00:08:28.232 --> 00:08:31.270
And the continuous convolution does not have an identity.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:35.801
So, it'd be kind of susque to use the 
 discrete version of the identity for convolution here.

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:38.552
If we can, we should just not use an identity.

00:08:38.552 --> 00:08:41.667
So first, we need to modify our algorithm.

00:08:41.667 --> 00:08:46.478
If you had an identity the kind of algebraic 
 structure we were working with before is called a monoid.

00:08:46.478 --> 00:08:49.238
But we can actually get away with even less.

00:08:49.238 --> 00:08:52.146
If there's no identity, like there isn't for convolution,

00:08:52.146 --> 00:08:53.686
then we can still do 'fast_pow'.

00:08:53.686 --> 00:08:55.654
We just need to change our base case.

00:08:55.654 --> 00:08:57.864
This is called a semi-group structure by the way

00:08:57.864 --> 00:09:02.427
if you just have associativity 
 and not necessarily even an identity.

00:09:02.426 --> 00:09:04.460
So we change our base case.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:07.323
We now no longer allow raising to the zeroth power.

00:09:07.323 --> 00:09:10.294
We just assume or raise 
 an exception if you want that

00:09:10.294 --> 00:09:13.387
the smallest power that 
 we're going to feed to this function is 1.

00:09:13.387 --> 00:09:15.513
x to the power one is just x.

00:09:15.513 --> 00:09:17.429
That one is always available.

00:09:17.428 --> 00:09:22.143
Once again, division here is division 
 of the power and the power is an integer.

00:09:22.143 --> 00:09:24.574
We no longer have to pass the identity.

00:09:24.574 --> 00:09:29.191
And voila. Our 'fast_pow' is now 
 complete and in its most general form.

00:09:29.191 --> 00:09:33.746
As long as you're not raising to the power zero, 
 this is still going to give all the same answers as before.

00:09:33.746 --> 00:09:36.744
But now we no longer have to specify an identity.

00:09:36.744 --> 00:09:39.698
Now let's get back to the convolution.

00:09:39.698 --> 00:09:43.279
We no longer need the identity.

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:44.822
Then I'm going to take some samples.

00:09:44.822 --> 00:09:49.750
And use a discrete approximation of 
 the uniform distribution between 0 and 1.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.735
Let's pull in matplotlib and plot it.

00:09:52.735 --> 00:09:55.502
When we plot it, we just see 
 a flat line which is what we expect

00:09:55.502 --> 00:09:57.323
because it's the uniform distribution.

00:09:57.323 --> 00:10:00.253
Every point is as likely as every other point.

00:10:00.253 --> 00:10:03.024
Now get ready for the very cool surprise.

00:10:03.024 --> 00:10:07.565
What do we get when we use 
 convolution as our multiplication?

00:10:07.565 --> 00:10:12.173
And raise the uniform 
 distribution to the 30th power?

00:10:12.173 --> 00:10:14.260
Are you ready?

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.242
Well, what do you know!

00:10:16.242 --> 00:10:19.500
Doesn't that look familiar? 

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.063
Doesn't that just look so much like a&nbsp;bell curve?

00:10:23.063 --> 00:10:24.865
And there's no tricks here.

00:10:24.865 --> 00:10:27.557
There's no e to the minus x squared anywhere&nbsp;inside.

00:10:27.557 --> 00:10:30.000
You can run the example yourself.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.943
Let's put that in a loop and see it go slowly.

00:10:33.943 --> 00:10:37.688
At n equals zero, we just see the uniform distribution.

00:10:37.688 --> 00:10:39.293
Then we see a triangle.

00:10:39.293 --> 00:10:44.761
And then each subsequent convolution seems 
 to give something more and more normal distribution like.

00:10:44.761 --> 00:10:48.820
Albeit, shifting further and 
 further to the right and getting squished.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:53.657
And the really cool fact is that this doesn't 
 even depend on using the uniform distribution.

00:10:53.657 --> 00:10:56.603
You could pick any initial distribution that you want.

00:10:56.603 --> 00:10:58.837
Here, I just took some random formula.

00:10:58.836 --> 00:11:03.674
2 * x minus x squared plus 1 for those X is bigger than 0.5

00:11:03.675 --> 00:11:05.751
minus 1 for those X's less than 0.1.

00:11:05.751 --> 00:11:08.271
So, this formula isn't even continuous.

00:11:08.271 --> 00:11:10.399
Divide out by the sum to make the sum equal to 1.

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:12.236
So it's a probability distribution.

00:11:12.236 --> 00:11:15.233
And then look at what happens 
 when we do the iterated convolutions.

00:11:15.233 --> 00:11:18.991
As you can see, the blue line, 
 the initial distribution wasn't even continuous.

00:11:18.991 --> 00:11:24.130
But after just a few iterations, we're seeing 
 that same normal distribution looking shape again.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.470
If you think this is cool, 
 you should really get into probability.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:30.613
The theorem I play here is the central limit theorem.

00:11:30.613 --> 00:11:33.960
And the reason that this 
 is somehow related to convolutions 

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:38.075
is that the distribution of a 
 sum of&nbsp;independent random variables

00:11:38.075 --> 00:11:41.135
is given by the convolution of their distributions.

00:11:41.135 --> 00:11:43.620
But that's maybe for a different video.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:45.481
In any case, I hope you liked "fast_pow".

00:11:45.481 --> 00:11:51.000
And importantly, I hope this shows you some 
 of the potential benefits of being&nbsp;able to take an algorithm.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.769
And reduce it down to its fundamental pieces.

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:57.115
Abstract away&nbsp;the details that you don't really care about.

00:11:57.115 --> 00:12:01.140
Is there some underlying structure there&nbsp;
 that makes sense in a more general context?

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.487
So while "fast_pow" itself is quite a&nbsp;
simple example.

00:12:04.487 --> 00:12:07.184
Some of the craziest, most useful results

00:12:07.184 --> 00:12:10.484
and very cool&nbsp;optimizations come from abstracting things

00:12:10.484 --> 00:12:12.999
in the same way that we did in this&nbsp;video.

00:12:12.999 --> 00:12:15.240
Usually with a little more math, though.

